        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If you are looking up the <strong>Social Security 2027 benefit increase</strong> for your state, the short answer is that the raise is the same everywhere: a <strong>3.6% cost-of-living adjustment (COLA)</strong>, announced by the Social Security Administration (SSA) on October 14, 2026. Searches about the Social Security 2027 benefit increase states map often expect different raises by state, but the COLA is a single national percentage. What does vary by state is the size of the typical check and whether the state taxes benefits. This guide gives a full 2027 COLA chart, spousal, disability (SSDI) and SSI amounts, and a state-by-state view. To check your exact amount, use our <a href="{tool:social-security-cola-calculator}">Social Security COLA calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed in October 2026</h2><ul><li><strong>3.6% COLA for 2027.</strong> The SSA announced on October 14, 2026 that benefits rise 3.6%, starting with January 2027 payments.</li><li><strong>Medicare Part B for 2027.</strong> As of October 24, 2026, the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services had not announced the 2027 Part B premium; it is normally published in the fall (the 2026 premium of $202.90 was announced in November 2025). Any increase is usually deducted from the raise.</li><li><strong>SSI and SSDI.</strong> The maximum federal SSI payment rises to $1,029 for an individual and $1,544 for a couple, and the SSA estimates the average disabled-worker benefit rises from about $1,642 to $1,701.</li><li><strong>Full retirement age 67.</strong> People born in 1960 or later have a full retirement age of 67, and the first of them reach it in 2027.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Social Security 2027 benefit increase states: is the raise different?</h2><p>No. The COLA is set by federal law and applies equally to every beneficiary in every state, territory and abroad. It comes from the change in the Consumer Price Index for Urban Wage Earners and Clerical Workers (CPI-W) between the third quarter of 2025 and the third quarter of 2026. There is no regional cost-of-living adjustment, so a retiree in Mississippi and a retiree in New Jersey with the same benefit get exactly the same dollar raise.</p><p>Three things do differ by state:</p><ul><li><strong>Average benefit size.</strong> Benefits follow lifetime earnings, so states with higher wages tend to have higher average checks. The SSA publishes state averages in table 5.J of its <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/policy/docs/statcomps/supplement/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Annual Statistical Supplement</a>. A larger average check means a larger average dollar raise, even though the percentage is the same.</li><li><strong>State income tax on benefits.</strong> Most states do not tax Social Security, but a handful do, as described below.</li><li><strong>State SSI supplements.</strong> Many states add a state supplement to the federal SSI payment, and those amounts are set by each state, not by the COLA.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>2027 Social Security payment increase at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>2027 figure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>COLA</td><td>3.6% (announced October 14, 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Average retired worker</td><td>$2,095 to $2,170 (about +$75)</td></tr><tr><td>Aged couple, both receiving benefits</td><td>$3,238 to $3,355</td></tr><tr><td>Average disabled worker (SSDI)</td><td>about $1,642 to $1,701</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum federal SSI, individual</td><td>$994 to $1,029</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum federal SSI, couple</td><td>$1,491 to $1,544</td></tr><tr><td>First payment with the raise</td><td>January 2027 (SSI: December 31, 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum taxable earnings</td><td>$192,300</td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Source: SSA 2027 COLA fact sheet and SSI federal payment amounts. Averages are SSA estimates.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>Social Security 2027 COLA increase chart</h2><p>The chart below applies the 3.6% COLA to monthly benefits from $800 to $4,000. Federal law (42 U.S.C. 415) rounds the increased primary insurance amount down to the next lower dime, and then rounds the monthly benefit actually paid down to the next lower whole dollar after deductions such as the Medicare premium. The "new benefit" column shows the amount rounded down to the dime; the "paid" column shows it rounded down to the dollar, before any Medicare or tax deductions. Your notice may differ slightly because the COLA is applied to your exact primary insurance amount, not to a rounded check.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Benefit in 2026</th><th>New benefit, 2027</th><th>Paid (whole dollars)</th><th>Raise per month</th><th>Raise per year</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
