            . '<tr><td>$3,200</td><td>$3,315.20</td><td>$3,315</td><td>$115.20</td><td>$1,382.40</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>$3,400</td><td>$3,522.40</td><td>$3,522</td><td>$122.40</td><td>$1,468.80</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>$3,600</td><td>$3,729.60</td><td>$3,729</td><td>$129.60</td><td>$1,555.20</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>$3,800</td><td>$3,936.80</td><td>$3,936</td><td>$136.80</td><td>$1,641.60</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>$4,000</td><td>$4,144.00</td><td>$4,144</td><td>$144.00</td><td>$1,728.00</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>If you want a printable version, such as a Social Security 2027 COLA increase chart PDF, use your browser’s print option and choose "Save as PDF". The figures above are the full table.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: what a retired couple keeps</h2><p>A married couple receives $1,850 (the higher earner) and $1,150 (the spouse) a month, $3,000 in total. Applying 3.6% and rounding down to the dime gives $1,916.60 and $1,191.40, a combined $3,108.00, which is $108 more a month or $1,296 a year. Suppose each has a Medicare Part B premium deducted. If the 2027 premium were, for example, $10 higher than in 2026 for each of them, $20 of the $108 would go to Medicare, leaving a net raise of $88 a month. The 2027 Part B premium is set by the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services, so check your December notice for the real figure. If the couple lives in a state that taxes benefits and their income is above its exemption, a small part of the remaining raise could also go to state tax.</p>'
            . '<h2>2027 Social Security COLA spousal benefits</h2><p>Spousal benefits get the same 3.6% increase. A spouse can receive up to 50% of the worker’s primary insurance amount if the spouse claims at their own full retirement age; claiming earlier reduces it, to as little as 32.5% at 62 for someone whose full retirement age is 67. For example, if a worker’s full benefit is $2,400, a spouse claiming at full retirement age would receive $1,200 in 2026 and $1,243.20 in 2027. A spouse who is also eligible on their own record receives their own benefit first, topped up by any spousal amount that is higher. Survivor benefits, which can be up to 100% of the deceased worker’s benefit, rise by 3.6% as well.</p>'
            . '<h2>Social Security COLA 2027 for disability (SSDI)</h2><p>The 3.6% COLA applies to Social Security Disability Insurance. The SSA estimates the average disabled worker’s benefit will rise from about $1,642 to $1,701 a month. Benefits for a disabled worker’s spouse and children rise by the same percentage. SSDI recipients should also note that the earnings thresholds used for disability work rules, such as substantial gainful activity, are updated each year; the SSA lists the 2027 amounts on its fact sheet.</p>'
            . '<h2>2027 SSI payment amounts</h2><p>Supplemental Security Income is a separate, needs-based program, and it also rises by 3.6%. The SSA applies the COLA to the yearly federal payment standard and rounds down to a multiple of $12, which gives a maximum federal payment of <strong>$1,029 a month for an individual</strong> and <strong>$1,544 for an eligible couple</strong> in 2027, up from $994 and $1,491 in 2026. Because January 1, 2027 is a holiday, the first SSI payment at the new rate is made on December 31, 2026. Actual SSI payments are often lower because countable income reduces them, and many states add a supplement.</p>'
            . '<h2>Which states tax Social Security benefits?</h2><p>Federal tax can apply to up to 85% of benefits depending on income. At the state level, most states do not tax Social Security at all. For tax year 2025, eight states still taxed some benefits: <strong>Colorado, Connecticut, Minnesota, Montana, New Mexico, Rhode Island, Utah and Vermont</strong>. Most of them exempt people below set income levels or above a certain age, so many retirees in those states owe nothing on their benefits.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Status and how it generally works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Colorado</td><td>Taxes benefits, but residents 65 and older can subtract all federally taxable benefits; from tax year 2025, people 55 to 64 can subtract all of them if AGI is at or below $75,000 ($95,000 joint), otherwise up to $20,000 (Colorado Department of Revenue).</td></tr><tr><td>Connecticut</td><td>Exempts benefits below set income levels and limits the taxable share above them.</td></tr><tr><td>Minnesota</td><td>Offers a subtraction that phases out at higher incomes.</td></tr><tr><td>Montana</td><td>Taxes benefits under its own rules, with income-based relief.</td></tr><tr><td>New Mexico</td><td>Exempts benefits for most residents below set income levels.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhode Island</td><td>Exempts benefits for people at full retirement age with income below a yearly limit.</td></tr><tr><td>Utah</td><td>Includes benefits in income but offers a credit that phases out at higher incomes.</td></tr><tr><td>Vermont</td><td>Exempts benefits fully below set income levels and partly above them.</td></tr><tr><td>West Virginia</td><td>Phase-out complete: the State Tax Department lists a 100% exemption from tax year 2026, after 35% in 2024 and 65% in 2025.</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>State rules change often, and income limits are updated each year. Kansas, Missouri and Nebraska stopped taxing Social Security in 2024. Before relying on this list for a 2026 or 2027 return, check your state revenue department’s current instructions. Our <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> estimates the federal side.</p>'
            . '<h2>When will I see the 2027 Social Security benefit increase?</h2><ul><li><strong>Social Security and SSDI:</strong> the raise starts with the January 2027 payment, which pays for December 2026. Payment dates depend on your birth date: the second, third or fourth Wednesday of the month for most people who started benefits after May 1997, or the 3rd of the month for others.</li><li><strong>SSI:</strong> December 31, 2026.</li><li><strong>Notices:</strong> the SSA posts COLA notices in my Social Security accounts and mails paper notices in December to people who have not opted out.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How to use the chart</h2><ol><li>Find your current gross benefit (before Medicare) on your 2026 notice.</li><li>Look up the nearest row, or multiply by 1.036 and round down to the dime.</li><li>Subtract any increase in Medicare premiums and any tax you have withheld.</li><li>Update your budget with the net amount using the <a href="{tool:monthly-budget-planner}">monthly budget planner</a>.</li></ol><p>To see how your check compares with others your age, read our guide to the <a href="/average-social-security-check-by-age/">average Social Security check by age</a>, and for the full background on the raise see the <a href="/social-security-2027-cola-increase/">Social Security 2027 COLA guide</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/social-security-2027-cola-increase/">Social Security 2027 COLA: 3.6% raise and what it means</a></li><li><a href="/average-social-security-check-by-age/">Average Social Security check by age: 62, 65, 67, 70</a></li><li><a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">How much you need to retire in 2026</a></li><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">Should I refinance my mortgage?</a></li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> Social Security Administration, <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/news/en/cola/factsheets/2027.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">2027 COLA fact sheet</a> (October 14, 2026) and <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/OACT/cola/SSIamts.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">SSI federal payment amounts</a>; <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/415" rel="noopener" target="_blank">42 U.S.C. 415</a> (rounding of benefits); SSA <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/policy/docs/statcomps/supplement/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Annual Statistical Supplement</a>; <a href="https://tax.colorado.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Colorado Department of Revenue</a>; <a href="https://tax.wv.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">West Virginia State Tax Department</a>. The COLA chart and couple example are our own calculations.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'Is the Social Security 2027 benefit increase different by state?', 'No. The 3.6% cost-of-living adjustment is set nationally and applies to every beneficiary in every state. Average benefits differ by state because they follow lifetime earnings, and a few states tax benefits, but the percentage raise is identical everywhere, from Alaska to Florida.' ],
            [ 'How much is the 2027 Social Security payment increase?', 'Benefits rise 3.6% in 2027. The SSA estimates the average retired worker gets about $75 more a month, from $2,095 to $2,170. A $1,000 benefit rises by $36 and a $3,000 benefit by $108, before any change in Medicare premiums or tax withholding.' ],
            [ 'Where can I find a free Social Security 2027 COLA increase chart PDF?', 'This page is free to view and includes the full chart, with new amounts for benefits from $800 to $4,000 in $200 steps, using the 3.6% COLA and the federal rule that rounds benefits down to the dime and the paid amount down to the dollar. There is no login or download required — save it as a PDF from your browser with Ctrl+P (or Cmd+P on a Mac) and choosing "Save as PDF."' ],
            [ 'How does the 2027 COLA affect spousal benefits?', 'Spousal benefits rise by the same 3.6%. A spouse claiming at full retirement age can receive up to 50% of the worker’s full benefit, so a spouse receiving $1,200 in 2026 would receive $1,243.20 in 2027. Survivor benefits also rise by 3.6%.' ],
            [ 'Does the 2027 COLA apply to Social Security disability?', 'Yes. SSDI benefits rise 3.6% starting with the January 2027 payment. The SSA estimates the average disabled worker’s benefit will rise from about $1,642 to $1,701 a month. Benefits paid to a disabled worker’s spouse and children rise by the same percentage.' ],
            [ 'What is the maximum SSI payment in 2027?', 'The maximum federal SSI payment is $1,029 a month for an individual and $1,544 for an eligible couple in 2027, up from $994 and $1,491. The first payment at the new rate arrives on December 31, 2026. State supplements and countable income change the actual amount.' ],
            [ 'Which states tax Social Security benefits?', 'For tax year 2025, eight states taxed some benefits: Colorado, Connecticut, Minnesota, Montana, New Mexico, Rhode Island, Utah and Vermont. Most exempt lower incomes or older residents. West Virginia finished its phase-out with a full exemption from tax year 2026. Check your state revenue department for current rules.' ],
            [ 'When does the 2027 Social Security benefit increase start?', 'The new amount begins with Social Security payments made in January 2027, which pay for December 2026. SSI recipients see the increase on December 31, 2026. The SSA sends notices in December and posts them in my Social Security accounts.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'should-i-refinance-my-car',
        'title'      => 'Should I Refinance My Car? When It Saves Money',
        'keyword'    => 'should i refinance my car',
        'desc'       => 'Should I refinance my car? A lower rate on the same term saves money, but a longer term or fees can erase it. See a break-even example and checklist.',
        'excerpt'    => 'A break-even test, worked example and checklist for deciding whether refinancing your car loan will actually save money.',
        'answer'     => 'Refinance your car if you can get a meaningfully lower rate without stretching the term or paying fees that cancel the savings. Example: moving a $22,000 balance with 48 months left from 11% to 7.5% cuts the payment by $36.67 a month and saves about $1,510 after $250 in fees, breaking even in about 7 months.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'Refinancing pays off when the rate drop is big and the term stays the same or shorter.',
            'Divide fees by the monthly saving to find the break-even point in months.',
            'Stretching the term can lower the payment yet raise the total you pay.',
            'Shop several lenders within a short window so the credit inquiries count as one.',
        ],
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-03 09:00',
        'content'    => '<p class="lead"><strong>Should I refinance my car?</strong> It usually makes sense when you can get a clearly lower interest rate, keep the same or a shorter term, and pay little in fees. It usually does not when the loan is nearly paid off, the car is worth less than you owe, or the new loan only lowers the payment by adding years. This guide gives a simple break-even test, a worked example with real numbers, and a checklist. To run your own figures, use the <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a> and the <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed in October 2026</h2><ul><li><strong>New Federal Reserve rate data.</strong> The G.19 Consumer Credit release of October 7, 2026 shows commercial banks averaging 7.54% on 60-month new car loans in August 2026 (preliminary), up from 7.14% in the second quarter.</li><li><strong>Car loan interest deduction.</strong> Under Public Law 119-21, the IRS says borrowers can deduct up to $10,000 a year of interest for 2025 through 2028 on a loan taken out after December 31, 2024 for a new, personal-use vehicle whose final assembly was in the United States, whether or not they itemize. The deduction phases out above $100,000 of modified AGI ($200,000 joint), the VIN must go on the return, and the IRS says refinanced amounts generally remain eligible. Used cars do not qualify.</li></ul><p>If your loan qualifies, a lower rate after refinancing also means less deductible interest, but the cash saving from a lower rate is larger than the tax value of the lost deduction, because a deduction only returns your tax rate on each dollar of interest.</p>'
            . '<h2>What does refinancing a car loan mean?</h2><p>Refinancing a car loan means taking out a new loan, usually from a different lender, to pay off your current one. The new lender sends the payoff to your old lender, becomes the lienholder on your title, and you make payments under the new rate and term. Nothing about the car changes. What changes is the interest rate, the number of months left, the monthly payment, and the total you will pay.</p>'
            . '<h2>Should I refinance my car loan? The quick test</h2><p>Answer four questions:</p><ol><li><strong>Is the new rate meaningfully lower?</strong> A drop of two percentage points or more on a sizable balance usually produces real savings. A drop of half a point on a small balance often does not cover fees.</li><li><strong>Will the term stay the same or get shorter?</strong> Compare total cost, not just the payment.</li><li><strong>Do the fees pay back quickly?</strong> Divide total costs by the monthly saving. If the break-even point is well before the loan ends, refinancing is likely worth it.</li><li><strong>Do you have equity?</strong> If you owe much more than the car is worth, many lenders will not refinance, and those that do may charge more.</li></ol><p>If all four answers favor refinancing, get quotes. If two or more do not, it is usually better to keep the current loan and pay extra toward principal when you can.</p>'
            . '<h2>What are average auto loan rates right now?</h2><p>The Federal Reserve’s <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g19/current/default.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">G.19 Consumer Credit release</a> of October 7, 2026 reports an average rate of <strong>7.54% on 60-month new car loans</strong> and <strong>7.17% on 72-month loans</strong> at commercial banks for August 2026 (preliminary figures). For comparison, the 60-month average was 7.65% for 2025 as a whole and 8.16% for 2024. These are averages of the most common rates banks offer; your own offer depends on your credit score, income, the car’s age and mileage, and the loan-to-value ratio. Used car loans usually carry higher rates than new car loans.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Period (G.19, commercial banks)</th><th>60-month new car loan</th><th>72-month new car loan</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2024 (year)</td><td>8.16%</td><td>8.29%</td></tr><tr><td>2025 (year)</td><td>7.65%</td><td>7.80%</td></tr><tr><td>Q1 2026</td><td>7.53%</td><td>7.53%</td></tr><tr><td>Q2 2026 (revised)</td><td>7.14%</td><td>6.97%</td></tr><tr><td>August 2026 (preliminary)</td><td>7.54%</td><td>7.17%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If your current loan is well above these averages, perhaps because you financed with weaker credit or took a dealer rate without shopping, refinancing is worth checking.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: the break-even calculation</h2><p>Suppose you owe <strong>$22,000</strong> on your car with <strong>48 months</strong> left at <strong>11% APR</strong>. Your payment is <strong>$568.60</strong> a month, and if you keep the loan you will pay $27,292.87 more, of which $5,292.87 is interest.</p><p>A credit union offers <strong>7.5% for 48 months</strong>, and title and registration changes cost <strong>$250</strong>.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Keep current loan</th><th>Refinance, 48 months at 7.5%</th><th>Refinance, 60 months at 7.5%</th><th>Refinance, 72 months at 10%</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monthly payment</td><td>$568.60</td><td>$531.94</td><td>$440.83</td><td>$407.57</td></tr><tr><td>Remaining interest</td><td>$5,292.87</td><td>$3,532.92</td><td>$4,450.09</td><td>$7,344.93</td></tr><tr><td>Total paid, including $250 fees</td><td>$27,292.87</td><td>$25,782.92</td><td>$26,700.09</td><td>$29,594.93</td></tr><tr><td>Saving vs keeping the loan</td><td>none</td><td>$1,509.95</td><td>$592.78</td><td>costs $2,302.06 more</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>On the same 48-month term, the payment drops by $36.67 a month. Divide the $250 in costs by $36.67 and the break-even point is about <strong>6.8 months</strong>. After that, every payment is pure saving, about $1,510 in total.</p><p>The 60-month option lowers the payment by $127.77, which is tempting, but because you pay interest for 12 more months the saving shrinks to about $593. The last column shows how refinancing can backfire: a 72-month loan at 10% has the lowest payment, $407.57, yet costs about $2,302 more than keeping the current loan. Lower payments are not the same as lower cost.</p><h3>When the saving is too small</h3><p>Now suppose you have only <strong>18 months</strong> left and owe <strong>$9,000</strong> at 11%. Refinancing to 7.5% for 18 months lowers the payment from $544.67 to $530.21, saving $260.20 in total. After $250 of fees you would be ahead by about $10. Late in a loan, most of the interest has already been paid, so refinancing rarely helps.</p>'
            . '<h2>When should I refinance my car?</h2><p>The best times are usually:</p><ul><li><strong>Your credit score has improved.</strong> A score that has risen since you bought the car, for example after paying down card balances, can qualify you for a lower tier. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">how to improve your credit score fast</a> explains what moves scores.</li><li><strong>You took dealer financing without comparing.</strong> A dealer-arranged rate can include a markup. Comparing a bank or credit union offer may show a better rate.</li><li><strong>Market rates have fallen.</strong> If average rates in the G.19 data are well below what you pay, ask for quotes.</li><li><strong>Early in the loan.</strong> Interest is front-loaded, so the first half of a loan offers the most savings.</li><li><strong>You need a lower payment to avoid default.</strong> Stretching the term costs more in total, but if the alternative is missed payments or repossession, a lower payment can be the right trade-off. Talk to your current lender first, since some offer hardship options.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How long should I wait to refinance my car?</h2><p>There is no federal waiting period, but in practice there are two limits. First, the title. After a purchase, the original lender must be recorded as lienholder on the title before another lender can pay it off and take its place. Depending on the state, this can take several weeks to a few months. Second, lender rules. Some lenders require the existing loan to be a few months old or to show a record of on-time payments before they will refinance it, and many set limits on the car’s age, mileage and the minimum balance. Ask each lender for its requirements. For most people, the practical answer is to wait until the title has been processed and you have made several on-time payments, then refinance as early in the loan as you can.</p>'
            . '<h2>How does refinancing affect your credit score?</h2><p>Applying usually triggers a hard inquiry, which can lower a score slightly for a short time, and opening a new account lowers the average age of your accounts. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-should-i-know-before-i-shop-for-auto-loan-at-a-bank-credit-union-dealership-or-other-lender-en-755/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">advises</a> keeping rate shopping within a short period, generally 14 to 45 days depending on the scoring model, so that multiple auto loan inquiries are treated as one. Many lenders also offer prequalification with a soft check, which does not affect your score. Over time, on-time payments on the new loan help your credit as much as payments on the old one did.</p>'
            . '<h2>What fees should you expect?</h2><ul><li><strong>Title and registration fees</strong> charged by your state to record the new lienholder. These vary widely.</li><li><strong>Lender fees</strong>, such as an origination or processing fee. Many auto refinance lenders charge none, but check.</li><li><strong>Prepayment penalty</strong> on the old loan. Most auto loans do not have one, but read your contract or ask your lender.</li><li><strong>Add-on products.</strong> If you bought GAP insurance or a service contract with the original loan, ask whether it ends when the loan is paid off and whether you are due a prorated refund. Do not buy new add-ons you do not need just because they are offered with the new loan.</li></ul><p>Add all of these to the break-even calculation. The CFPB’s <a href="https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/documents/201606_cfpb_auto-loan-worksheet.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">auto loan shopping sheet</a> helps compare offers line by line. If you use the car for business, also check whether the interest on either loan qualifies for a deduction — see our guide to <a href="/car-loan-interest-deduction/">car loan interest tax deduction rules</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Can you refinance with negative equity?</h2><p>Negative equity means you owe more than the car is worth. In the example above, if the car were worth $18,000, the $22,000 loan would be 122% of its value. Many lenders cap loan-to-value at around 100% to 125% or decline the loan altogether, and higher loan-to-value usually means a higher rate. The CFPB <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/should-i-trade-in-my-car-if-its-not-paid-off-en-2045/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">warns</a> that rolling negative equity into a new loan makes it more expensive, because you pay interest on the old debt too. Options include paying the gap down in cash before refinancing, making extra principal payments until you have equity, or keeping the current loan. Our guide on <a href="/negative-equity-car-loan-how-to-get-out/">how to get out of a negative equity car loan</a> walks through each, and the <a href="{tool:negative-equity-car-loan-calculator}">negative equity calculator</a> shows the cost of rolling it over.</p>'
            . '<h2>When refinancing hurts</h2><ul><li>The new loan adds years, so total interest rises even at a lower rate.</li><li>The loan has less than about a year left.</li><li>Fees take longer to recover than the time left on the loan.</li><li>The car is old or high-mileage and the new lender charges a higher rate for it.</li><li>You are about to apply for a mortgage, when a new account and inquiry could matter. Check your <a href="{tool:debt-to-income-ratio-calculator}">debt-to-income ratio</a> first.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How to refinance a car loan, step by step</h2><ol><li><strong>Get your payoff amount</strong> and current rate from your lender, and check your contract for any prepayment penalty.</li><li><strong>Check your credit reports</strong> for errors and know your score.</li><li><strong>Estimate the car’s value</strong> to see your loan-to-value ratio.</li><li><strong>Get quotes</strong> from at least three lenders, including a credit union, within about two weeks.</li><li><strong>Compare total cost</strong> on the same term with the <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a> and the <a href="{tool:loan-payoff-calculator}">loan payoff calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Close the loan</strong>, sign the new documents and keep paying the old loan until you confirm it has been paid off.</li><li><strong>Confirm the payoff and title transfer</strong> with your old lender about a week later.</li></ol>'
            . '<p>If you are still choosing a car rather than refinancing one, our guide on <a href="/cash-back-or-low-apr-car-deal/">cash back or low APR</a> and the <a href="{tool:cash-back-vs-low-apr-calculator}">cash back vs low APR calculator</a> help you start with a better rate.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">Should I refinance my mortgage? A break-even guide</a></li><li><a href="/negative-equity-car-loan-how-to-get-out/">How to get out of a negative equity car loan</a></li><li><a href="/cash-back-or-low-apr-car-deal/">Cash back or low APR: which car deal is better?</a></li><li><a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">How to improve your credit score fast</a></li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g19/current/default.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">G.19 Consumer Credit</a>, released October 7, 2026 (terms of credit, commercial banks); Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/auto-loans/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">auto loan resources</a>, including guidance on rate shopping and negative equity. The worked examples are our own calculations using standard amortization.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'Should I refinance my car?', 'Refinance if you can get a meaningfully lower rate, keep the same or a shorter term, and recover any fees well before the loan ends. In our example, cutting 11% to 7.5% on $22,000 over 48 months saves about $1,510 after $250 of fees. Avoid refinancing that only lowers the payment by adding years.' ],
            [ 'When should I refinance my car?', 'Good times are after your credit score has improved, when you took dealer financing without comparing offers, when market rates have dropped, and early in the loan when most interest is still ahead. Refinancing late in a loan rarely saves much because most interest has already been paid.' ],
            [ 'How long should I wait to refinance my car?', 'There is no federal waiting period, but the original lender must be recorded on the title first, which can take weeks to a few months, and some lenders want a few months of on-time payments. In practice, refinance once the title is processed, as early in the loan as possible.' ],
            [ 'Should I refinance my car loan if it lowers my payment?', 'Only if the total cost also falls or you need the lower payment to avoid falling behind. In our example, a 72-month loan at 10% cut the payment to $407.57 but cost about $2,302 more than keeping the original loan. Compare total paid, not just the monthly amount.' ],
            [ 'Does refinancing a car hurt your credit?', 'It can cause a small, temporary dip from a hard inquiry and a newer account. The CFPB advises keeping auto loan shopping within roughly 14 to 45 days so multiple inquiries count as one. Prequalification with a soft check does not affect your score, and on-time payments rebuild it.' ],
            [ 'Can I refinance my car with negative equity?', 'Sometimes. Many lenders limit how much they lend compared with the car’s value, and higher loan-to-value means higher rates. You can pay the gap down in cash, make extra principal payments until you have equity, or keep the current loan. Rolling negative equity forward increases total interest.' ],
            [ 'What is the average auto loan rate now?', 'The Federal Reserve’s G.19 release of October 7, 2026 shows commercial banks averaging 7.54% on 60-month new car loans and 7.17% on 72-month loans in August 2026, preliminary figures. Used car loans and borrowers with lower credit scores usually pay more.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
